steno - Plover's typing engine in C, for the Moonlander
=======================================================

SYNOPSIS

    steno [-qv] [-c CONFIG] [-p PORT]  read the keyboard, type into X
    steno -T                           paper tape
    steno -S                           suggestions
    steno -TS                          both, side by side (-ST: the
                                       suggestions on the left)
    steno -l WORD|OUTLINE              look it up
    steno -a OUTLINE TRANSLATION       add a stroke
    steno -A                           add strokes, asking for each
    steno -W                           add a word: its sound, the
                                       outlines made for it, stroke
                                       yours (a dwm scratchpad, Mod+y)
    steno -G WORD|-                    the same as text (- : a word a
                                       line on stdin)
    steno -g [-x]                      dotterel-generated.dict: the rules'
                                       outline for every word (-x: the
                                       old dictionaries out of the list)
    steno -C DIR FILE...               Plover's dictionaries in steno's
                                       format, into DIR
    steno -t [-c CONFIG]               strokes from stdin, print what
                                       would be typed (for tests)
    steno -K                           stuck keys: let go of a Shift,
                                       Ctrl, Alt or Super still down,
                                       Caps Lock off (dwm: Mod+BackSpace)

DESCRIPTION

steno does what Plover does while you type, and nothing else: it reads
strokes from the Moonlander's Gemini PR serial port, looks them up in
Dotterel's dictionaries and types the text into X. It follows Plover
5.4.2's own code step by step (translator, formatter, orthography), so
the same strokes give the same text; on random corpora of 9,000
strokes the output is the same as Plover's, byte for byte.

It is one static binary (about 5 MB, Plover's word list included) and
needs nothing but the kernel and a running X: no Python, no Qt, no
libX11 (it speaks the X protocol itself, through XTEST). It starts in
about 0.06 s and uses about 13 MB, where Plover takes about 0.7 s and
110 MB; a stroke takes a few microseconds.

What Plover's plugins did for Dotterel is built in:

    Gemini PR, reconnecting by itself when the keyboard comes back
    plover-modal-dictionary (.mode files)
    plover-stitching, plover-last-translation ({*+}, {*!} ...)
    Dotterel's UK/US switch (#SU)

and so are three of Dotterel's dictionaries, which are more program
than words: their data is C (dotterel.c), so nothing is read or
parsed for them. A dict line names them without an extension:

    dotterel-movement   movement mode (dotterel-movement.modal)
    dotterel-modifiers  KHR, SH, THRA, KPW and the rest
                        (dotterel-modifiers.py); the keys come from
                        dotterel-punctuation, -commands,
                        -fingerspelling, -numbers and -movement, as
                        in the list (or their .dict files)
    dotterel-raw        raw mode, RA*U (dotterel-raw.json)

The dictionaries are Dotterel's, in steno's own plain format:
./install.sh steno clones the dotterel repo into
~/.system/steno/dotterel and updates it every run. steno needs
nothing of Plover's: to use Plover dictionaries of your own, turn them
into steno's format once with steno -C.

    steno            in a terminal: types, and shows the status screen
    steno &          in the background: types, and says nothing but
                     problems, on stderr
    steno -v         also say each stroke

STATUS SCREEN

Started in the foreground of a terminal, steno shows what it is doing
in a small screen, in the look of btop:

    +-+steno+-------------------------------------------------------+
    | keyboard    connected  /dev/ttyACM0                           |
    | X           connected  :0                                     |
    | doing       typing                                            |
    | last        KAT  cat                                          |
    | since start 212 strokes, 10 dictionaries, 130831 entries      |
    +-+q quit+-+o output+-+t tape+-+s suggestions+-+b both+-+A add..+
    +-+dictionaries+------------------------------------------------+
    |> 1 on  ~/.system/steno/user.dict    3  dict, -a adds .. |
    |  2 on  dotterel-base.dict            113816  dict             |
    |  3 off dotterel-affixes.dict            602  dict             |
    |  ...                                                          |
    +--+j/k choose+-+J/K move+-+space on/off+-+a add+-+d remove+----+
    +-+messages+----------------------------------------------------+
    | keyboard on /dev/ttyACM0                                      |
    +---------------------------------------------------------------+

A dictionary in the dictionaries directory shows by its file name
alone; one anywhere else (the user dictionary) shows its path.

"doing" is typing, output off, raw mode, movement mode (Dotterel's
.mode dictionary) or nothing (no keyboard). The messages are what
steno would say on stderr.

    j k, Up Down   choose a dictionary (g, G: the first, the last)
    J K, Shift+Up Shift+Down
                   move it up or down: the higher one wins
    space          turn it on or off (in raw mode: for when it ends)
    a              add one: a name in the dictionaries directory, or
                   a path; it goes last (Enter adds, Esc stops)
    d              take it out of the list (y to be sure); the file
                   stays
    r              read again the dictionary files that changed
    A              add a stroke: type its outline, Enter (the messages
                   say what it gives now), then its text, Enter; it
                   goes into the user dictionary, as steno -a (Esc
                   stops)
    o              output on or off, as PLOVER:TOGGLE
    t, s, b        open the tape, the suggestions or both in a window
                   of their own (steno -T, -S, -TS in the "terminal"
                   setting), to put next to what you write
    q, Ctrl+C      quit steno

Every change to the list is written to the settings file at once (its
dict and user lines, in the new order, where the first of them was;
the rest of the file stays). ~/.system/config/steno/config is a link into
this repo, so the change shows in git diff; the link stays a link.

OPTIONS

    -c FILE   settings (default ~/.system/config/steno/config)
    -p PORT   the serial port; auto (default) tries /dev/ttyACM0 to
              ttyACM9, every second until the keyboard is there
    -T        show the paper tape
    -S        show the suggestions
    -TS       show both, the tape on the left (also -T -S)
    -ST       show both, the suggestions on the left (also -S -T)
    -l X      what outline X gives in each dictionary, and how text X
              is written (shortest first); for an outline of several
              strokes, what each stroke writes alone, which is why it
              is written so (high + draw + lick: hydraulic). STROKE?
              is a stroke that writes nothing alone; an outline
              that is no entry but still types something (KAT/-S:
              cats, a suffix on a word) says what it types. Text is
              found as you'd type it: don't finds the U+2019 one, " the
              quotes, and a symbol the entry that writes it (. finds
              {.}, ( finds {~|(^}). A word no dictionary has gets the
              ways to write it that work, as in the suggestions
              (unboxing: UPB/PWOBGS/-G; color: KHROR/#SU; 250:
              #-B/#-PB/#E)
    -a O T    add outline O -> T to the user dictionary; says first
              what O gives now and how T is written already (or that
              they are new), then what it added and where. As in
              Dotterel, straight quotes in T are saved typographic
              (' as U+2019, " as U+201C or U+201D), but in a bare
              symbol, {#key}, {&...} or =...
    -A        the same, asking for one outline and translation after
              another (empty outline: done). To stroke the outline,
              turn on raw mode (RA*U) and back off for the text.
    -W        the word adder, below
    -G W      what -W shows for word W, as text: syllables, sounds,
              each outline made for it, why, and what it types now
    -g        make dotterel-generated.dict, below; -x with it also takes the
              old dictionaries (dotterel-old-*) out of the list
    -C D F..  make steno's files in directory D from Plover's: .json
              -> .dict, .modal -> .mode, uk_to_us.json -> uk_to_us.txt
              (the three built-in ones are skipped)
    -t        test mode: one stroke or outline a line on stdin; prints
              B n (backspaces), S text, K combo
    -q        no status screen, even in a terminal
    -v        verbose: say each stroke
    -h        help

SETTINGS

One a line, # for comments (see config, which install.sh links to
~/.system/config/steno/config):

    port auto|PATH           the keyboard
    dictionaries DIR         where dict names are looked for
    user FILE [off]          a dictionary, and where -a and -A write;
                             made empty when it isn't there
    dict NAME [off]          a dictionary (.dict or .mode, below,
                             or a built-in one); the first wins
    data DIR                 Dotterel's data (uk_to_us.txt); default
                             DIR/../data of dictionaries
    undo N                   strokes * can undo (100)
    start_capitalized yes|no
    start_attached yes|no
    command NAME SHELL...    {PLOVER:NAME} runs SHELL with /bin/sh
    terminal COMMAND         what t, s and b on the status screen open
                             steno -T, -S, -TS with (st -e)

The commands Plover itself knows: {PLOVER:TOGGLE}, RESUME, SUSPEND
(each also forgets what was typed, for undo) and QUIT.

A dictionary that changes on disk (steno -a, or an edit by hand) is
read again at the next stroke; one that can't be read keeps its old
contents.

DICTIONARIES

Plain text, one entry a line: the text, a tab, then every outline that
writes it, separated by spaces. No JSON, nothing nested:

    cat         KAT
    {^}.ca      #KRA #KRA* KRA*

(a tab after the text, not spaces). In the text, \t is a tab, \n a
newline, \r a carriage return and \\ a backslash; empty lines are
skipped. When an outline is on two lines, the last one wins.

    NAME.dict   TEXT <tab> OUTLINES
    NAME.mode   option <tab> exclude_entry|exit_on_mismatch|
                    exit_on_match|ignore_folding <tab> yes|no
                entry|dict|exit <tab> TEXT <tab> OUTLINES
                (plover-modal-dictionary's tables and switches)

Dotterel's are in its repo, in steno/ (made from the JSON by its
steno/make.sh, which runs steno -C). ./install.sh steno links them
into ~/.system/steno, or, when they are older than the JSON,
makes them there and says so.

PAPER TAPE AND SUGGESTIONS

Each is its own box, drawn as btop draws them: steno -T is the tape,
steno -S the suggestions, steno -TS both, side by side (one above the
other in a terminal under 60 columns), the tape first; steno -ST puts
the suggestions first. So they can go in one window
or in two, wherever there is room.

    +-+tape+--------------------------++-+suggestions+----------------+
    |         O     PB        on      ||on                            |
    |  T   H                  this    ||  -PB  AUPB  OPB              |
    |                                 ||on this                       |
    |                                 ||  THOPB                       |
    +---------------------------------++--+quit+-+clear+-+tape+-+sugg..+

The tape: each stroke in the steno order's columns and what it wrote,
as it is in your document (Could, not could, after a full stop; a
return as a U+21B5 arrow). A stroke that writes no text says what
it does: (capital next), (small letter next), (join), the keys it
pressed (Control_L(BackSpace)). When a later stroke makes one outline
of the strokes before it, their text goes and the last one shows the
word, so you see what was
written:

    H AO EU     high          H AO EU
    TK  RA U    draw    ->    TK  RA U
                              HR EU B G   hydraulic

An undone stroke and its * leave the tape (or stay, dim, with u),
and what it had cleared comes back. A suffix stroke (-D, -G ...)
shows the word it made: PRE/SKRAOEUB, -D gives prescribed on the -D
line, the earlier lines cleared as in an outline (and SKRAOEUBD, the
suffix in the stroke, shows scribed, not scribe {^ed}). The same for
strokes that change what is there: #* (KAT: cath), SP-LS (cat hat:
cathat) and #SU (colour: color) clear the lines they changed and show
the word on their own line; a prefix stroke ({for^}) shows for, and
the next word shows the word they made (forsending), # (repeat) shows
what it wrote again, and a stroke that does nothing shows no text. One
* takes back what steno takes back: a stroke that writes nothing (KPA,
which only capitalises the next word) goes together with the stroke
before it, and so do the tape's lines. Fingerspelling the same way, as
you spell: the newest letter's line shows the word so far (s, so, som,
some ...) and the letters before it are cleared.

The suggestions: the words you typed, and under each every outline
that writes them, shortest first (fewest strokes, then fewest keys);
each words once (typed again, they move to the bottom). The newest is
at the bottom in both. A word is what the strokes that wrote it
wrote, from a space, a quote or a bracket on: o'clock is one word,
We've after an opening quote is one, a prefix ({for^}) waits for the
word it joins, and strokes that are no entry give none.

Every word keeps, in bold, the outline you wrote it with: green when
no outline of that text has fewer strokes (the same number is right
too), red when one has. It is in the list where its length puts it
(added when the dictionaries don't have it), and the mark stays until
you write that word again. It is the strokes that wrote the word,
whatever the dictionary has: KAT/-S for cats (red: KATS), KAT for Cat
after KPA, KHROR/#SU for color.

A word no dictionary has gets the ways to write it that work instead:
a word and a suffix (TPEURG/-Z, or the suffix in the stroke: TPEURGZ),
a prefix and a word (TPOR/SEPBD), or letter by letter (P*P/R*/*EU
...), and an American spelling the British word and the UK/US switch
(KHROR/#SU). Each is tried in a copy of steno, so only ones that type
the word are shown, and your undo history isn't touched.

When a stroke takes the word before in (pre, then prescribe), puts a
suffix on it, is a fingerspelled letter (each step in place of the one
before) or a *, the lines for the words that are gone go too.

    q, Ctrl+C          quit
    t                  show or hide the tape
    s                  show or hide the suggestions
    c                  clear both
    j k, Down Up       scroll a line (the title says how far up)
    Ctrl+D Ctrl+U,     half a screen
    PageDown PageUp
    G g, End Home      the newest (follow again), the oldest
    mouse wheel        scrolls the box under it, 3 lines
                       (Shift and the mouse still select text)
    u                  undone strokes and their * gone from the tape
                       (the start), or there and dim, as in Plover
    Tab                with both: which one scrolls (its title is lit)
    x                  with both: swap them (left and right, or top
                       and bottom)
    l, /               look up a word or outline: type it, Enter; the
                       box shows each outline and why (steno -l) and
                       the keys are the window's again: l looks up
                       another, Esc closes it
    a                  add a stroke: type its outline, Enter (the box
                       says what it gives now, or that it is no
                       outline, to mend), then its text, Enter;
                       it goes into the user dictionary, as steno -a
                       (Esc stops)
    any other key draws it again

At least one box stays. Into a pipe, it writes the lines instead
(suggestions start with "= " when the tape is there too).

The engine sends them through two FIFOs, $XDG_STATE_HOME/steno/tape
and suggest (mode 0600, in a 0700 directory; steno -W adds outline,
ask and lastword), each only while a viewer
has it open; with the suggestions hidden or not shown anywhere, it
doesn't look for them. One viewer of each at a time. Nothing is
written to disk. Whatever you type passes through them, so they are
yours only; don't show them where others see the screen.

GENERATED DICTIONARY (steno -g)

steno -g makes dotterel-generated.dict in the dictionaries directory,
beside the others and named as they are: every word of Dotterel's word
list (data/word-homes.txt) that goes in base or proper-nouns, about
54,000, with the first outline the rules of steno -W make for it, the
most common words first (data/frequency.txt), each outline once (a
word whose outlines are all taken gets one with a star). It is made
with every dictionary off, so it is the rules alone; proper nouns get
# on the first stroke, as in Dotterel. It takes about 10 s.

The first time, it goes last in the list, so all your dictionaries
stay and win over it; run it again after the rules change to make it
anew. Your dictionaries are never changed. steno -g -x also takes the
old dictionaries (dotterel-old-*) out of the list (the files stay; "a"
on the status screen puts one back). Restart steno to use the new
list.

ADDING WORDS (steno -W)

Mod+y in dwm (the Word key on the Moonlander's dwm layer) opens it as
a scratchpad: st -t stenoword -e steno -W. It asks the running engine
for the last word you wrote, so fingerspell a word, open it and the
word is there, looked up. A capital that only starts a sentence goes
(She: she) unless data/word-homes.txt says it is a proper noun;
^K switches the case.

A full stop you type stays part of the word: Mr., e.g., Dr. In the
last word written, one at the end goes as a sentence's (cat.: cat),
but not from an abbreviation the word list or a dictionary knows, as
it is or as Dotterel writes a title (Mr.{-|}), or one with a full stop
inside too (e.g.). A word with braces is taken as it is: Mr.{-|}
saves the title that capitalizes the next word.

    +-word------------------------------------------------------------+
    | > beautiful                                                     |
    | adjective    goes to dotterel-base.dict    common #578          |
    +-sound-----------------------------------------------------------+
    | written       beau|ti|ful    3 syllables                        |
    | UK  spoken    /'bu: | tI | f@l/   BU:-ti-fel    beau | ti | ful |
    | US  spoken    /'bu | t@ | f@l/                                  |
    | meaning       adjective delighting the senses ...               |
    +-made by the rules---------++-lapwing-----++-your dictionaries---+
    |PWAOU/TEU/TPUL  write-out  ||PWAOUFL      ||PWAOUFL   old-base   |
    |PWAOUT/-FL      yours ...  ||PWAOUT/-FL   ||PWAOUT/-FL old-base  |
    +---------------------------++-------------++---------------------+
    +-why PWAOU/TEU/TPUL----------------------------------------------+
    | beau -> PWAOU  long u, oo: AOU                                  |
    +-outline: stroke it----------------------------------------------+
    | > _                                                             |
    +-----------------------------------------------------------------+

(the real screen has Habamax colours, IPA and Cyrillic). The sounds
are RP (Britfone) and American (CMUdict); a word only CMUdict has gets
its RP made from the American, one neither has is guessed from the
spelling, and the box says so. The outlines made by the rules come
from the sound and the spelling, as Dotterel and Lapwing write them:
long vowels by sound, short ones by spelling, the chords on the right,
then the shorter ways (folded clusters, -le, R, L, E and T folding, a
dropped vowel, a word plus an ending, two words with a star). Each
says why and whether it is free; one already taken gets an AE or a
star version too. A proper noun's outlines (its dictionary is
proper-nouns, ^T changes it) get # on the first stroke.

"your dictionaries" also lists the outlines that only dictionaries
you turned off have (space in the status screen), dim and marked
(off): KA*T old-base (off). They type nothing now, so the rules count
them as free, but they say how you used to write the word; choose one
and Enter saves it again where the word belongs. steno -l WORD lists
them too, under "is written in dictionaries that are off", and -l
OUTLINE marks such a dictionary "(file, off)".

When the outline you stroke or type is one a list has (the rules',
Lapwing's or your dictionaries', the ones turned off too), it turns
green, so does that row (on a green bar, the whole width), and the
line under it says which lists have it.

While the outline box has the focus, your strokes come here instead
of being typed: the engine sends them through the FIFO "outline"
while steno -W holds it open (the window has to have X's focus: st
tells it). Then:

    Enter          save the outline (none stroked: the chosen one)
                   and quit; a stroke that writes Enter does it too
    Esc            back to the word, to type another (Esc again quits)
    * or ^W        take the last stroke back
    ^U             clear       ^Y  take the chosen outline
    Up/Down, ^N/^P choose      Tab, Shift+Tab  the next list
    ^T             another dictionary for it
    ^D             take the chosen outline out of your dictionary
    ^K             the other case (Bill, bill)
    ^F, ^O         hear the word: the Cambridge Dictionary's British
                   (^F) or American (^O) sound, played with mpv (F and
                   O are next to each other on Canary's top row)

Both sounds start downloading in the background as soon as a word is
looked up, apart from playing, so they are usually in before you ask.
^F and ^O play at once whenever a sound is in (a new press stops the
one playing); pressed while it is still coming, it plays as soon as it
is in. When a sound can't be had (Cambridge has none, or the download
failed), the message line says so.

The sounds are fetched through Tor only (proxychains, ./install.sh
net), since each fetch tells Cambridge a word you are adding; without
proxychains nothing is fetched. The page's sound links and each mp3
are kept for good in ~/.system/steno/sound, so a word goes out once; delete the
directory to forget them.

It saves into dotterel-NAME.dict in the dictionaries directory, NAME
from data/word-homes.txt (base, proper-nouns, affixes, punctuation,
numbers, commands, fingerspelling; a word not in it: proper-nouns
with a capital, affixes for {^...}, else base), or user.dict (^T).
steno reloads it at once.

PRIVACY

No network (but ^F and ^O in steno -W, through Tor, above), no log
file, no history kept on disk: what you typed lives in memory for undo
(100 strokes) and is gone when steno stops. -v prints strokes to
stderr; leave it off where stderr is kept.

KNOWN DIFFERENCES FROM PLOVER

    {=...} (Plover's custom transformers) and retro currency
        ({*($c)}) are not there.
    Upper and lower case of letters outside Latin-1, Latin
        Extended-A, Greek and Cyrillic are left as they are.
    A word longer than 500 bytes is cut.
    TOGGLE, RESUME and SUSPEND forget the history (Plover keeps it).

FILES

    main.c       settings, commands, the serial port, the main loop
    dict.c       strokes, dictionaries, modal and modifier dictionaries
    dotterel.c   Dotterel's movement, modifiers and raw mode, as data
    translate.c  Plover's translator: longest match, undo, macros
    format.c     Plover's formatter: spacing, case, metas
    ortho.c      orthography: a small regex engine and Plover's rules
    x11.c        the X protocol: XTEST, the keymap, spare keys
    tools.c      the tape and suggestion feeds, -l, -a, -A, and what
                 -W asks the engine for
    gen.c        -G: sounds, syllables and outlines made by the rules
    word.c       -W: the word adder's screen
    sound.c      -W's ^F ^O: Cambridge's sounds, through Tor, kept
    view.c       -T, -S, -TS: the boxes
    tui.c        the status screen
    screen.c     drawing boxes in the terminal, for both
    mkwords.c    builds words.c from words.txt at build time
    words.txt    Plover's American English word list (for orthography)
                 (dict.c also reads Plover's JSON, only for -C)
    test/        make test: a small corpus checked against Plover

